                <pb facs="0023" n="16" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg16"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/13953: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-01-27T11:00:00+08:00">January 27, 1937—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:19 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>46. My 44, January 26, 3 p.m.</p>
                <p>In a conversation with the Minister for Foreign Affairs January 26, 5 p.m., the
                    latter confirmed that the efforts of the Chinese Government to consolidate its
                    authority in Shensi Province centered mainly on an attempt to separate the
                    forces of Yang Hu Cheng and the Northeast Army from association with the
                    Communist forces. He said that the trend of the discussions warranted optimism.
                    He denied that the Red forces exceeded 50,000 in number and insisted that their
                    suppression would have been accomplished by now if they had not obtained support
                    from the two rebel forces.</p>
                <p>In spite of the vague optimism expressed by the Minister for Foreign Affairs, the
                    Embassy has not been able to learn of any concrete advance in the
                    reestablishment of the Government’s authority in Sian.</p>
                <p>Sent to Peiping, Hankow, Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
